\mt1 Editor’s Introduction
\ip This volume brings together two English translations that have long been read apart. The Old Testament is Sir Lancelot Brenton’s English translation of the Septuagint, as printed in 1870. The New Testament is the King James Version in the text prepared by F. H. A. Scrivener for the Cambridge Paragraph Bible of 1873. Both texts come from eBible.org, and Brenton’s includes the corrections eBible has made to it.
\ip The Septuagint is the Old Testament of the Orthodox Church, and it is, as Brenton says in his preface, “the Old Testament translated into the language of the New.” Most English Bibles translate the Old Testament from the Hebrew, so a reader seeking the Greek must look elsewhere. Brenton wrote in the style of English of the King James Bible, which has also been used to translate the services of the Church. The two testaments thus speak with one voice. The name Orthodox Liturgical English Bible reflects these choices: Orthodox for its Old Testament and the order of its books, which follows the Church of Greece; liturgical for its English.
\ip Each testament opens with its translation’s prefaces and introductions. Brenton’s preface and introduction come before Genesis, along with an introduction to the Apocrypha written for a later edition after his death in 1862. The Epistle Dedicatory and The Translators to the Reader stand before Matthew. Scrivener’s critical introduction and appendices were never part of the online text and are not printed here. Brenton’s list of abbreviations follows the table of contents, supplemented with others used in the notes. His notes and supplied passages follow Revelation. The opinions expressed in these pages are those of their original authors.
\ip The books that English Bibles set apart as the Apocrypha are printed in their Greek places, so there is no separate Apocrypha section: the Prayer of Manasses follows 2 Chronicles, 4 Maccabees follows 3 Maccabees, and the additions to Esther and Daniel and Psalm 151 stand within the text. The introduction’s account of each book or section is printed as a footnote where it begins. Words in square brackets are the editor’s. Brenton’s single book of Ezra and Nehemiah is divided into 2 Esdras and Nehemias, and {brenton MAL 3:19-24} is numbered {bare MAL 4:1-6}, as in the Greek. Most books keep their Septuagint names. Others are renamed to follow the same practice: Brenton’s Joshua is Jesus, the Son of Navi, and his four books of Kings are the four books of Kingdoms. Hosea and Nahum take their familiar English names in place of his Osee and Naum.
\ip The translations are preserved with a few exceptions. Brenton translated the Vatican text and gave readings from Codex Alexandrinus in his notes and Appendix. Where Swete’s edition supports the wording and placement of a reading, it is printed in the text. The displaced reading appears in a footnote, marked “Vat.” where that attribution is supported and “Brenton’s text” elsewhere. Doubtful readings remain where he left them. The missing verse at {1SA 23:12} is supplied from the King James Version. The wording of the New Testament is unchanged.
\ip Brenton’s footnotes are printed, with a few slips corrected. In the notes and the front and back matter of both translations, citations use this edition’s book names and verse numbers. Abbreviations and numbers follow The Chicago Manual of Style; the wording is otherwise unchanged. The online text of the New Testament has no notes, so it carries instead the marginal notes of the King James Bible of 1611, as transcribed by Calvin George, without their cross-references. Words that the translators supplied to complete the English sense, which many Bibles print in italics, are set here in ordinary type. The title pages, the table of contents, and the headings are this edition’s own.
\ip Where the New Testament quotes the Old, a link at the first verse of each passage names the full passage at the other end. The link also records Turpie’s judgment (1868) on the quotation’s wording: “Heb. + LXX” where it follows the Hebrew and the Septuagint alike, allowing differences in wording. In “Heb. ≠ LXX” and “LXX ≠ Heb.”, it follows the first text named and differs from the second. This meaning applies at both ends of the link. A link without a gloss marks a quotation that he judged to follow neither, or to depart from both by whole clauses, as when a verse is abridged or two passages are joined. Turpie compares the Greek and Hebrew texts, rather than the two English translations.
\ip Links use Brenton’s chapter and verse numbers, which sometimes differ from the King James Bible’s. Brenton numbers the psalms as the Greek does, most of them one lower, and he counts the title of a psalm as its first verse or two, so that {PSA 33:13-17} is the King James Bible’s {kjv bare PSA 33:13-17}. From chapter 25 on, the chapters of Jeremias follow a different order, so that {JER 38:31-34} is the King James Bible’s {kjv bare JER 38:31-34}. Where the Septuagint adds to a verse, Brenton may set the addition apart as a lettered verse, which the King James Bible lacks: the cheerful giver of {2CO 9:7} is found at {PRO 22:8a}. The table that follows this introduction gives the King James Bible’s number wherever it differs, and takes the place of Brenton’s table of the chapters of Jeremias.
\ip The page size is ISO B5 (176 × 250 mm). Scripture is set in one column in Utopia at 10 pt, with the notes in the inside margin. The design is based on the Berean Standard Bible layout that comes with PTXprint, a free program for typesetting Bibles.
